OSP Bundle Administration Utility
Open Service Platform

!!! Introduction

The <!BundleAdmin!> utility is a web-based application for managing, installing and upgrading bundles in an OSP application.
The BundleAdmin utility is itself implemented in a bundle named <[com.appinf.osp.bundleadmin]> and requires the Web bundle
to function.

The following features are provided by the BundleAdmin utility:

  - list all bundles installed in an OSP application
  - get detailed information (version, dependencies) for installed bundles
  - manage the lifecycle of bundles (install, resolve, start, stop, uninstall, upgrade)
  

!!! Accessing the BundleAdmin Utility

The BundleAdmin utility can be accessed from anywhere on the network using a web browser.
Since the BundleAdmin utility runs within the OSP web server,
the TCP port number on which the OSP web server runs must be known. The default port for the
OSP web server is 22080, and the default port for the secure (HTTPS) web server is 22443.
Within the OSP web server, the BundleAdmin utility lives under the path <[/bundleAdmin]>.
Assuming a default installation running on your local host, the BundleAdmin utility can be 
accessed by directing your browser to <http://localhost:22080/bundleAdmin>. 

Access requires a user with the permission <[bundleAdmin]>, which the auth service checks.
With the SimpleAuth bundle, the administrator user (<[auth.simple.admin.name]>, "admin" by
default) has every permission. Its password is set with <[auth.simple.admin.passwordHash]>,
which has no default: no login succeeds until it is configured.
See the [[OSPAuth.html AuthService documentation]] for other ways to manage users.

The pages log in users with HTTP Basic authentication. To log in users in a web session instead
(with a login form of another bundle), set the bundle property <[web.session]> to the name of
that session, for example in an extension bundle.

A request that changes the state of a bundle (any button on the pages) must be a POST request
from a page of the same server: its Origin header (or, without one, its Referer header) must
name the server, and in a web session it must carry the CSRF token of the session, which the
forms of the pages contain. A script that sends such requests must send one of the two headers.
Other requests are answered with 403 Forbidden. Requests to stop, uninstall or upgrade the
BundleAdmin bundle, or a bundle it depends on, are answered with 409 Conflict.
A request that uploads a bundle file must state its length (otherwise 411 Length Required), which
must not exceed the bundle property <[upload.maxSize]> (256 MB by default; otherwise 413 Request
Entity Too Large).
The pages accept only GET, HEAD and POST requests; other methods are answered with
405 Method Not Allowed.

Upon successful login, the BundleAdmin utility shows a list of all bundles installed in the
current application:

